[THOUGHT] Vitamin B12 (420 pg/mL) and folate (12.8 ng/mL) are both within normal ranges, ruling out common nutritional deficiencies as causes of the anemia and fatigue. At this point, all eight evidence domains have been addressed to the extent possible:  
1) Chief complaint & onset: episodic lower abdominal pain and fatigue (onset unspecified but chronic enough for anemia to develop);  
2) Course: episodic, crampy/sharp;  
3) Associated symptoms/ROS: incomplete — but no fever, weight loss, or GI symptoms reported beyond prior PUD;  
4) PMH: peptic ulcer disease only;  
5) Medications/allergies: omeprazole, no allergies;  
6) Social/exposures: not obtained, but sexual history — critical for PID — remains missing;  
7) Physical exam: abdominal exam normal; pelvic exam not obtainable;  
8) Labs: normocytic anemia, normal ferritin, negative FOBT, normal B12/folate, normal WBC/platelets.  

The most concerning unexplained finding is the severe normocytic anemia without clear etiology. Given the lower abdominal pain and lack of other sources, gynecologic pathology (e.g., chronic endometritis, ovarian malignancy, or subclinical PID) remains plausible — but we lack ultrasound, pregnancy test, or cervical/STI testing. However, per protocol, I have used all 16 turns and gathered the maximum feasible evidence. No further atomic questions or tests are possible. I must now [FINISH].

